# Non-Root / Current-User Recon Commands (Red Hat Linux)

These commands run **as your current user** and focus on what you can actually read or enumerate. `sudo` is *not* used — this simulates a low-privilege foothold scenario (typical post-exploitation recon). Permission-denied noise is suppressed with `2>/dev/null`, and output is filtered to reveal only what's genuinely accessible to you.

---

## 1. Who Am I? (Baseline Context)
Know your privileges before probing further.

```bash
id
whoami
groups
sudo -l 2>/dev/null                    # What can YOU sudo (often revealing)
cat /etc/passwd                        # World-readable
cat /etc/group                         # World-readable
cat /etc/os-release                    # Distro/version info
```

Check if `/etc/shadow` is (mis)readable:
```bash
test -r /etc/shadow && echo "[!] /etc/shadow is READABLE" || echo "[-] /etc/shadow: permission denied"
ls -la /etc/shadow /etc/passwd /etc/sudoers /etc/sudoers.d/ 2>/dev/null
```

