## 9. Local Services / Panels (Without Root)
`ss -p` **cannot** show process names for other users' processes without root. Use `ps` (which by default shows all processes) and `ss` for port mapping.

```bash
# Listening sockets — works as non-root (no process names for other users)
ss -lntu
# Add process info only for your own processes:
ss -lntup 2>/dev/null

# All processes + their listening ports via /proc (best non-root method)
ps -eo user,pid,ppid,args --sort=user | head -100
ps aux

# Map PID → listening port where possible (own processes or world-readable /proc/*/fd)
ss -lntp 2>/dev/null
lsof -i -P -n 2>/dev/null | grep LISTEN

# Look for admin panels / known service signatures
ps -eo user,pid,cmd | grep -Ei 'apache|httpd|nginx|php-fpm|tomcat|jenkins|gitlab|portainer|grafana|kibana|adminer|phpmyadmin|webmin|cockpit|jupyter' | grep -v grep

# Docker / containers (may reveal exposed panels)
docker ps 2>/dev/null
docker ps --format '{{.Names}}\t{{.Ports}}' 2>/dev/null
ls -la /var/run/docker.sock 2>/dev/null   # Check socket perms
```

### Probe each discovered port for a web panel
```bash
for p in $(ss -lnt | awk '/LISTEN/{print $4}' | awk -F: '{print $NF}' | sort -u); do
  echo "=== Port $p ==="
  curl -sk -m 3 -o /dev/null -w "HTTP %{http_code}  %{url_effective}\n" "http://127.0.0.1:$p/" 2>/dev/null
done
```

